<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=chemical-weapons-in-world-war-i">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chemical weapons in World War I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as poison gas in World War I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">Chemical weapons</a> were used on a large scale for the first time in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The French Army was the first to use tear gas, in 26 mm grenades in August 1914. Germany fired shells of xylyl bromide on the Rawka River on 31 January 1915 and used chlorine against French troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> on 22 April 1915. Phosgene and mustard gas followed. Estimates reach about 90,000 deaths and 1.3 million casualties, and the 1925 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Protocol was signed after the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons-in-world-war-i.jpg" alt="Chemical weapons in World War I" width="330" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flanders_WWI_gas_attack.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>August 1914</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/fritz-haber">Fritz Haber</a> · <a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The first large-scale use came on 31 January 1915 (German xylyl bromide shells at Bolimov) and the first lethal agent was German chlorine, released on 22 April 1915. A letter dated 2 January 1915 about chlorine is discounted as probably misdated.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/grenade">Hand grenade<span>1044</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">Synthetic dye<span>1856</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chemotherapy">Chemotherapy<span>December 1942</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=chemical-weapons-in-world-war-i">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
